\begin{array}{ll} r_0=6\\z_0=36\end{array}
The upper boundary of \mathcal{W} is the plane z=36, and the lower boundary is z={x}^2+{y}^2={r}^2. Therefore, {r}^2\le z\le 36.
The projection \mathcal{D} onto the xy-plane is the circle {x}^2+{y}^2=36 or r=6. That is, \mathcal{D}: \,0\le \theta \le 2\pi,\, 0\le r\le 6 The inequalities defining \mathcal{W} in cylindrical coordinates are thus \mathcal{W}: \,0\le \theta \le 2\pi,\, 0\le r\le 6,\, {r}^2\le z\le 36 Therefore, we obtain the following integral: \int\!\!\int\!\!\int _{\mathcal{W}} z \,dV= \int_0^{2\pi } \int_0^{6} \int_{{r}^2}^{36} zr \,dz \,dr \,d\theta= \int_0^{2\pi } \int_0^{6} \frac{{z}^2r}{2}\bigg|_{z={r}^2}^{36} \,dr\,d\theta=\int_0^{2\pi } \int_0^{6} \frac{r(1296-{r}^4)}{2} \,dr \,d\theta= \int_0^{2\pi } \int_0^{6} \frac{{1296}r-{r}^5}{2} \,dr \,d\theta= \int_0^{2\pi } \frac{1296 {r}^2}{4}-\frac{{r}^6}{12}\bigg|_0^{6} \,d\theta=7776\int_0^{2\pi } \,d\theta = 15552\pi
